# 8. Task Status

Every task follows the same lifecycle.

```
Draft

↓

Ready

↓

Assigned

↓

In Progress

↓

Implementation Complete

↓

Review

↓

QA

↓

Ready for Merge

↓

Merged

↓

Released

↓

Closed
```

Alternative states:

- Blocked
- Cancelled
- Deferred

---

# 9. Assignment Rules

Tasks may be assigned only when:

- status is Ready;
- dependencies are satisfied;
- Knowledge Package exists;
- owning module exists;
- required documents are identified.

Agents must never self-assign work.

Assignment is performed by the Project Manager or orchestration system.

---

# 10. Dependency Rules

Every dependency must be explicit.

Supported dependency types:

- Blocks
- Blocked By
- Requires
- Related
- Parallel With

Circular dependencies are prohibited.

---

# 11. Task Priority

Priority reflects business urgency.

| Priority | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| P0 | Critical |
| P1 | High |
| P2 | Normal |
| P3 | Low |

Priority must not be confused with complexity.

---

# 12. Complexity

Complexity estimates implementation effort.

| Level | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| XS | Less than 2 hours |
| S | 2–4 hours |
| M | 4–8 hours |
| L | 1–2 days |
| XL | Multi-day |
| XXL | Must be decomposed |

Tasks estimated as XXL must never be assigned.

---

# 13. Readiness Level

Every task records its implementation readiness.

Possible values:

- Planned
- Ready
- Assigned
- Implementation Complete
- Integration Ready
- UAT Ready
- Production Ready

Readiness follows the definitions in:

**55-module-definition-of-done.md**

---

# 14. Required Evidence

Every completed task must produce evidence.

Minimum evidence includes:

- implementation summary;
- changed files;
- executed tests;
- review outcome;
- readiness level;
- documentation updates;
- rollback confirmation.

Tasks without evidence cannot be closed.

---

# 15. Required Reviews

Every implementation task identifies mandatory reviewers.

Typical reviewers:

- Backend Review
- Database Review
- QA Review
- Security Review
- Documentation Review
- Architecture Review

Review requirements depend on module risk.

---

# 16. Documentation Updates

Every task must explicitly answer:

```
Does this implementation require documentation updates?

YES / NO
```

If YES, the affected documents must be listed.

Implementation is not complete until documentation has been updated.

---

# 17. AI Agent Rules

Implementation Agents must:

- load the Knowledge Package;
- remain inside allowed files;
- follow Coding Standards;
- follow Definition of Done;
- produce implementation evidence.

Agents must never:

- invent Product Decisions;
- modify unrelated modules;
- silently expand task scope;
- ignore review requirements.

---

# 18. Human Review Rules

Human reviewers verify:

- Blueprint compliance;
- Engineering compliance;
- implementation quality;
- security;
- maintainability;
- documentation;
- readiness level.

Human review remains the final approval authority.

---

# 19. Task Traceability

Every task must maintain traceability to:

- Epic
- Capability
- Feature
- Module
- Blueprint
- Engineering
- ADR
- API
- Event
- Database
- Tests
- Pull Request
- Release

Traceability must remain valid after project completion.

---

# 20. Task Metrics

Recommended metrics include:

Planning:

- Planned Tasks
- Ready Tasks
- Blocked Tasks

Execution:

- Active Tasks
- Review Tasks
- QA Tasks

Delivery:

- Completed Tasks
- Reopened Tasks
- Failed Tasks

Quality:

- Review Pass Rate
- QA Pass Rate
- Rollback Rate
- Defect Escape Rate

---

# 21. AI Task Generation

Implementation tasks should be generated automatically from:

- Product Backlog
- Module Implementation Package

Generated tasks should already contain:

- Knowledge Package
- Blueprint references
- Engineering references
- Allowed Files
- Forbidden Files
- Review Agents
- Mandatory Tests

This minimizes manual project management effort.

---

# 22. Task Archival

Completed tasks are never deleted.

Archived tasks remain available for:

- audits;
- analytics;
- release history;
- production investigations;
- architectural evolution.

Historical tasks remain immutable.

---

# 23. Governance

The Project Manager owns:

- prioritization;
- assignment;
- scheduling.

The Solution Architect owns:

- technical scope;
- dependencies;
- implementation approach.

Implementation Agents own execution.

QA owns validation.

Documentation Agent owns documentation consistency.

---

# 24. Definition of Project Task Catalog Ready

The Project Task Catalog is considered ready when:

- task identifiers are standardized;
- categories are defined;
- lifecycle is defined;
- ownership rules are defined;
- dependency rules are defined;
- evidence requirements are defined;
- review process is defined;
- AI execution rules are defined;
- traceability rules are defined;
- governance rules are defined;
- archival policy is defined.

The Project Task Catalog becomes the authoritative operational register for all implementation work after Blueprint Freeze v1.0.